# Saltwell (Docs/Design/83-dialogue-trees.md, "Saltwell's own speech"): the oasis hamlet of the Red Waste, by its spring
# pool on the way to the Saltpan; salt rakers, the Ochre vines' hands, warreners, a store and the Oasis Inn, and the
# carters who come in off the sand. A dignified, unhurried speech of hospitality: peace and welcome first, the guest
# honoured and the host's water and shade praised; courteous to strangers, warm to its own ("my brother", "my
# sister"); fond of proverbs. Its world is the well and the spring, salt, the caravans and carts, dunes and wadis, the
# noon heat and the cool of night, date palms, shade, the stars a traveller steers by, and trade done by measure and
# oath ("by salt and water": salt shared is a promise kept). The broad accent is the more formal, never another
# grammar: a longer blessing, one courtesy more.

grammar saltwell
greet = Peace on you | Water and shade to you | Be welcome
greet[here.time=dawn] = Peace with the morning | Peace on you, and a cool morning
greet[here.time=dusk|night] = A cool evening to you | Peace on you, and a cool night
greet.broad = Peace on you, and on your house | Peace on you, and water, and shade
farewell = Go in peace | Peace on your road | Water on your way
farewell.broad = Go in peace, and come back to us | Peace on your road, and stars to steer by
thanks = May your well never fail | My thanks, and water to you | You are generous
thanks.broad = May your well never fail, and your shade never thin
agree = It is so | Truly said | As sure as the spring
refuse = That well is dry | Not for all the salt in the pan | Not by any measure
surprise = Stars above | By the spring | Who would have said it
annoyed = Sand in my teeth | Dust and thorns | A dry well take it
very = truly
good = sweet | sound
bad = brackish | sour
worthless = dust | blown sand
luck = a full cistern | a sweet well
child = palm-shoot | little one
pet!! = light of my eyes | my star
money = silver | coin
cold = night-cold | cold as the stars
hot = noon-hot | hot as the Saltpan
rain = sky-water | rain
tired = sun-weary | worn as a well rope
drunk = wine-merry | full of the Ochre red
nonsense = mirage-talk | wind in the dunes
tag = , is it not | , not so
oath! = By salt and water | By the well and the stars | By bread and salt
home = my own well | my father's roof
# (Phase 7, the rest of plain's words.)
address = my brother^m | my sister^f | friend of the well
address[topic.meeting=stranger] = honoured guest | traveller
address[other.stage=young] = little one | palm-shoot
bed = my mat | the cool of my bed
dear! = light of my eyes | my sweet water | my heart
hungry = empty as a dry well | hungry
hurt = sore | knocked about | aching
little = a grain of sand | a sip
robbery = That's robbery under the noon sun | Short measure and a thief's weight
saying! = The well remembers who drew from it | Every caravan comes home at last | Salt keeps, and so does a promise | Shade is for sharing
work = the day's labour | the work
# (Phase 7, the conversations' words.)
wages = silver | the day's silver
boss = the pan-master | the head of the well
busy = busy as the well at dusk | never still
slow = still as noon | quiet
takings = the day's silver | the till
cheated = short-measured | sold a skin of sand
mum! = Mother | Mama
dad! = Father | Baba
gran = Grandmother | Old Mother
grandad = Grandfather | Old Father
supper = the evening bread | supper
toffs = the great Houses | the silk-pawed
rabble = the dust-pawed lot | that lot
skint = dry as the Saltpan | empty-pursed
posh = silken | grand
busybody = well-talker | gossip
fool = you sun-addled thing | you empty water-skin
daft = sun-addled | moon-struck
toast = Water and shade | To the well | Long life and sweet water
drink = a cup of date wine | a cup of the Ochre red
ale = date wine | the Ochre red
fog = dust-haze | haze
snow = snow | frost
mud = dust | sand
wind = dust-wind | a wind off the dunes
watch = the Watch | the well-watch
thief = night-taker | thief
bandits = dune-raiders | road wolves
gaol = the cells | the strong-room
dole = the alms-bread | the well-share
grave = the sand | the long shade
grub = bread and dates | food
tasty = sweet as dates | good
snug = cool as a courtyard | shaded
beasts = the goats and hens | the flock
bet = a wager | a wager of dates
poorly = sun-sick | poorly
chill = a night-chill | a touch of sun-fever
remedy = salt in sweet water | honey and date syrup | a wet cloth in the shade
olddays = the old caravan days | the years of the old well
blessing = Peace on you | Water and shade be yours | May your well run sweet
bless = Peace keep you | Sweet water keep you | The Bright One shade you
someday = one cool day | when the Waste blooms
far = many wells off | beyond the dunes | three wadis over
stranger = guest | road-stranger
journey = crossing | caravan road
# The dialogue window (doc 83).
shop_offer = Look, and take your time: my shelves are yours. | What may I bring you? | Sit, look. Nothing is hurried here.
shop_dear = Everything comes over the sand, and the sand charges. | Dear as water in the dry season. | The caravans set the prices, not I.
shop_sold = May it serve you well. | Weighed true, and yours. | Fairly measured, by my oath.
shop_bye = Go in peace, and come again. | Peace on your road. | May your water skin stay full.
shop_bare = The last caravan took the best of it. | My shelves are as dry as the pan. | Little left until the carts come.
talk_open = Sit, sit. What is on your heart? | Speak, and be at ease. | What brings you to my shade?
# A giver's work (doc 83, Phase 2).
work_offer = There is work, if you will carry it in the cool hours. | I could use honest paws. | Something wants doing before the heat.
work_taken = Good. Go at first light and keep to the shade. | Good. Your word is your salt. | It is agreed, then, by measure and by word.
work_paid = Here, by measure, as promised. | Paid in full, and my thanks with it. | Weighed true. You have earned it.
work_back = No shame in it. Another will carry it. | So be it. The road is long for everyone. | Better said now than at the bottom of the well.
work_none = Nothing today. The well is quiet. | No work until the next caravan. | Nothing that needs doing just now.
# The other roles (doc 83, Phase 3).
inn_offer = Come in out of the sun. A bed, bread or something cool? | Rest your feet. There is a bed, and bread, and cool water.
inn_bed = Up the stairs, where the night air comes in. There's a chest for your things. | Your room is cool and made up. Sleep in peace.
watch_greet = The watch. Keep the peace of the well and we'll get on. | Speak, traveller. The watch is listening.
church_greet = Peace on you. Come in out of the heat. | Come in. The shade here is for everyone.
learn_offer = It is a trade that keeps water in the jar. | Honest work, and measured fairly.
# The dialogue trees' other words (doc 83, Phase 4): news and gossip there isn't, a trouble that isn't, thanks for
# help, the way, a resident's work, work turned down, an inn's fire and its helpers, the watch's word, a master's
# new apprentice, something to hand over; a keeper asleep.
shop_asleep = Mm... the heat's gone... come back in the cool of the morning. | Zzz... the shutters are closed. Morning, in peace.
news_none = Nothing new since the last caravan. | Quiet as noon. | The road has brought no news.
gossip_none = Talk at the well stays at the well. | Nothing I would repeat.
trouble_none = Nothing that shade and water won't mend. | Nothing, and thank you for asking.
trouble_thanks = May your well never fail. I won't forget this. | You have been generous. I am in your debt.
way_ask = Where are you bound, traveller? | Where to? I'll point you true.
job_busy = This and that, as the day allows. | I work in the cool hours and rest in the heat.
work_declined = As the guest wishes. | Another day, then, in peace.
inn_rest = Sit by the fire, then: the night comes cold off the sand. A bed upstairs rests you better. | Take the bench by the hearth. Nobody moves a guest.
watch_noted = Noted, and by my oath they'll answer for it. | Good. The watch owes you a cup of cool water.
watch_bye = Go in peace, and keep it. | Move along, then, in peace.
learn_taken = Good. Come at first light, before the heat, and stay near me. | Then you are mine to teach. Keep close, and watch my paws.
receiver_ask = Something for me? Come into the shade. | For me, traveller?
inn_helper = I only help here. Ask #here.innkeeper#: the rooms are theirs to give. | #here.innkeeper# keeps this house. I carry the water.
